1.  
- **Title:** ‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and A 
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia  
- **URL:** 
https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-hellenic-studies/article/ring
-of-nestor-a-glimpse-into-the-minoan-afterworld-and-a-sepulchral-treasure-of-gol
d-signetrings-and-beadseals-from-thisbe-boeotia/5E465BD1031E9251C962E138498E6CFA
  
- **Publisher/Author:** Cambridge University Press; article in *The Journal of 
Hellenic Studies* (author: Sir Arthur J. Evans)  
- **Date:** Published online 23 December 2013 (originally 1925 in JHS volume 45)
- **Source type:** Academic journal article  
- **Triggered by query:** `"Ring of Nestor gold signet ring Pylos Thisbe 
Boeotia"` and `"Ring of Nestor signet ring Pylos museum collection"`  
- **Relevance:**  
  - Foundational scholarly study that coined the term “Ring of Nestor” for a 
particular large gold signet ring from a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near 
Thisbê in Boeotia.  
  - Describes discovery circumstances (1915 find by a peasant near the village 
of Dombrena), tomb context, associated grave goods (gold signet rings, bead 
seals, bronze spearheads, sword), and detailed iconography of the ring’s 
engraved scenes.  
  - Discusses dating (late Mycenaean), stylistic and cultural connections to 
Minoan glyptic art, and interpretive issues about the scenes and their possible 
relation to beliefs about the afterlife.  
- **Read content / faithful summary:**  
  - The article presents a hoard of gold signet rings and bead-seals discovered 
in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê, Boeotia. The discovery, made under
wartime conditions and initially outside official archaeological channels, was 
later documented by Arthur Evans, who was able to study and exhibit the entire 
treasure to the Society (JHS).  
  - Evans identifies one particularly large and elaborate gold signet ring whose
engraved designs are organized in three successive scenes on the bezel. Because 
of its scale, quality, and narrative imagery, he labels it “the Ring of Nestor.”
The ring’s hoop is described as a beaded gold band, and the bezel carries 
miniature relief scenes that Evans interprets as reflecting Minoan beliefs about
the afterworld.  
  - The article details the tomb’s contents: multiple gold rings, bead-seals in 
gold, bronze spear-heads, and a short sword of a very late Mycenaean type. These
finds are used to date the burial and place it within the broader evolution of 
Mycenaean funerary practices.  
  - Evans compares the Ring of Nestor’s form and decoration with other Minoan 
and Mycenaean signet rings, highlighting the technical skill of the engraver and
the fusion of Minoan iconography with Mycenaean funerary contexts. He proposes 
that the scenes may illustrate a passage to the afterworld and explores possible
literary resonances with Homeric Nestor, although his discussion remains 
speculative and tied to contemporary early 20th‑century interpretations.

