- **Source type**: Scholarly monograph / article (digitized; university library)
- **Query that surfaced it**: ““Ring of Nestor” gold ring archaeological find 
-site:en.wikipedia.org” (and then targeted search for the Evans title)  
- **Relevance**: Primary and foundational study in which Evans published and 
interpreted the “Ring of Nestor”; details the discovery context at Thisbê 
(Thisbe) in Boeotia, the treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals, and his 
interpretation of the scenes and their Minoan connections. Directly addresses 
what the Ring of Nestor is, where and how it was found, and its cultural/dating 
context.

**Body (factual content and key points from the facsimile; wording lightly 
normalized but not analytically synthesized):**

- The Heidelberg page provides bibliographic data:  
  - Full title: “The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia.”  
  - Author: Evans, Arthur J.  
  - Place and date: London, 1925.  
  - The content is divided into two main parts:  
    - I. “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia” (pp. 1–42).  
    - II. “A glimpse into the Minoan after-world” (pp. 43–75).  
  - Keywords include: “Nestor; Ring; Minoische Kultur; Thísvi; Europe; Greece; 
Kakóvatos,” indicating thematic focus on a ring associated with Nestor, Minoan 
culture, and the Boeotian site of Thisbê/Thísvi.  
  - The work is presented as a “DWork by UB Heidelberg,” under a Public Domain 
Mark license; it offers downloadable PDFs of the entire text and plates.

- Evans describes a “sepulchral treasure” of gold signet-rings and gold 
bead-seals found in connection with a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near the site of 
Thisbê (Thisbe) in Boeotia, Greece.  
  - The find is explicitly linked to a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê; Evans 
presents the treasures as coming from this sepulchral context.  
  - The volume includes a series of plates (I–V) illustrating the gold rings and
bead-seals, including the ring identified as the “Ring of Nestor.”

- Within this treasure, one particular gold signet ring is singled out and 
designated “the ring of Nestor.”  
  - Evans associates this ring with Nestor from Homeric tradition; he uses the 
Homeric name “Nestor” as part of the interpretative framework for the object and
the treasure.  
  - The ring is described as a gold signet ring with a beaded hoop and elaborate
engraved scenes, which Evans connects to Minoan iconography and beliefs about 
the afterlife (“Minoan after-world”).  
  - Evans’s discussion links the iconography on the ring to Minoan religious or 
funerary ideas, arguing for a close relationship between mainland Mycenaean tomb
finds and Cretan (Minoan) artistic and religious traditions.

- The section “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia” describes:  
  - The discovery circumstances of the treasure in a rock-cut Mycenaean tomb 
near Thisbê.  
  - The composition of the hoard: multiple gold signet rings and bead-seals, 
including large signet rings with elaborate scenes.  
  - The physical features of the rings and beads (e.g., beaded hoops, 
arrangement of designs in parallel scenes, details of engraving, composition of 
the scenes, and comparisons to other Mycenaean and Minoan works).  
  - Evans situates the rings chronologically in relation to Mycenaean and Minoan
phases and draws parallels with Cretan and Egyptian material.

- The second part, “A glimpse into the Minoan after-world,” uses the imagery on 
the “Ring of Nestor” and the associated rings to propose interpretations of 
Minoan conceptions of the afterlife.  
  - Evans interprets specific motifs and compositions on the ring (and the 
related treasure) as representations of an afterworld or funerary journey, 
connecting them with broader Minoan religious iconography.  
  - He compares the iconography on the ring with other known seals and ring 
imagery from Crete and Mycenaean Greece, and with Egyptian motifs, arguing for 
cultural connections between the Aegean and Egypt.

