- The article contributes to ongoing scholarly debates about:  
  - The cultural attribution of the ring (Minoan, Mycenaean, Egyptianizing, or 
later).  
  - The dating of the piece (bronze age vs later reproduction/forgery).  
  - The extent of Egyptian influence on Aegean art and the possibility of modern
or early-modern imitations.

---

### Source 7

- **Title**: “[PDF] Discovery of the Royal Signet-ring; Beaded gold hoop 
resembling that of ‘Ring of Nestor’; Parallel arrangement of designs—three 
successive scenes; Epilogue Part I – The Royal Signet-ring”  
- **URL**: 
https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/02D0
2A39B4DE198280F05F8F75972A9E/9781139795326c21_p947-956_CBO.pdf/epilogue-part-i-t
he-royal-signet-ring.pdf  
- **Author / Publisher**: Published by Cambridge University Press; content 
appears to be from an epilogue (“The Royal Signet-ring”) in a scholarly volume 
on Aegean or Mycenaean archaeology (exact author name not present in snippet; 
likely a modern scholarly author discussing Aegean signet rings).  
- **Date**: Not specified in snippet; likely 20th or 21st century.  
- **Source type**: Scholarly book chapter / article (PDF via Cambridge Core)  
- **Query that surfaced it**: ““Ring of Nestor” gold ring archaeological find 
-site:en.wikipedia.org”  
- **Relevance**: Discusses a royal gold signet ring with a beaded hoop 
resembling that of the “Ring of Nestor.” Provides comparative data on form and 
design and helps situate the Nestor ring among other high-status signet rings, 
including features like beaded hoop, parallel scenes, and multiple designs.

**Body (from snippet and inferred context):**

- The PDF is titled “Epilogue Part I – The Royal Signet-ring,” and within that 
section, a passage mentions:  
  - “Discovery of the Royal Signet-ring; Beaded gold hoop resembling that of 
‘Ring of Nestor’; Parallel arrangement of designs—three successive scenes.”  
- This text describes:  
  - A royal signet ring whose beaded gold hoop is explicitly compared to the 
hoop of the Ring of Nestor.  
  - The arrangement of its engraved designs in three successive scenes, i.e., 
multiple panels or vignettes arranged in sequence, similar to how some 
descriptions characterize the Nestor ring’s design layout.  
- The author uses the Nestor ring as a reference standard for describing 
stylistic and formal traits of another royal signet ring, reinforcing:  
  - The Nestor ring’s distinctive beaded hoop and multi-panel engraved design.  
  - Its role as a benchmark in scholarship on Aegean/Mycenaean gold signet 
rings.

