      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "This Journal of Hellenic Studies article by Arthur J.
Evans presents and analyzes a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and 
bead-seals discovered in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê 
in Boeotia. Evans describes how a peasant near the village of Dombrena found the
hoard under wartime conditions, and how the objects later came to scholarly 
attention, allowing him to examine and display them. He gives a detailed 
inventory of the tomb contents, including multiple gold rings, gold bead-seals, 
bronze spearheads, and a short sword of very late Mycenaean type, using these 
associated finds to date the burial. Among the rings, he singles out a 
particularly large and elaborate gold signet ring, which he names the \"Ring of 
Nestor,\" characterized by a beaded gold hoop and a large bezel engraved with 
three successive miniature relief scenes. The article focuses on the iconography
of these scenes, which Evans interprets as representations of Minoan beliefs 
about the afterworld and the soul's journey after death, and compares the ring's
style and technique with other Minoan and Mycenaean glyptic works. He argues for
strong stylistic and cultural connections between Cretan and mainland workshops 
and cautiously explores potential literary resonances with the Homeric figure of
Nestor, while acknowledging the speculative nature of such links.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "This digitized monograph by Arthur J. Evans, hosted 
by the University of Heidelberg, is an extended version of his study on the 
so-called Ring of Nestor and the associated sepulchral treasure from a Mycenaean
chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia. The text sets out the circumstances of 
discovery of the rock-cut tomb near the village of Dombrena and provides 
systematic descriptions of each gold signet ring and bead-seal in the hoard. 
Evans devotes particular attention to a large gold signet ring that he calls the
Ring of Nestor, describing its beaded hoop, large rectangular or oval bezel, and
the arrangement of engraved scenes in separate panels. He analyzes the ring's 
construction and compares its form and engraved narrative layout to other major 
Aegean gold rings, such as the so-called Ring of Minos, suggesting possible 
shared workshop traditions or stylistic lineages. A substantial section 
discusses the iconography of the Ring of Nestor in relation to Minoan religious 
concepts about the afterlife, proposing that the scenes depict stages in the 
soul's journey or encounters with divinities. The monograph includes five plates
reproducing photographs and drawings of the Ring of Nestor and related objects, 
making it a key visual and textual reference for later scholarship on the 
dating, stylistic attribution, and interpretive significance of the ring and its
funerary context.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
