      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "Jack L. Davis's article \"The Lord of the Gold 
Rings\" in Hesperia, accessible via Academia.edu, examines the 2015 discovery of
the so-called Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos and 
situates its finds within the broader tradition of Aegean Bronze Age gold signet
rings. Davis describes the Griffin Warrior grave, a Late Helladic IIA burial 
near Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos, which contained hundreds of artifacts including 
four gold signet rings with Minoan-style ritual scenes. He analyzes the 
iconography of these rings, arguing that their imagery derives from Minoan 
religious motifs and that their presence in a Mycenaean mainland burial reflects
close cultural interaction between Crete and Mycenaean elites. The article 
places the Griffin Warrior rings in a longer history of Aegean gold signet rings
by comparing them to well-known pieces, including Evans's Ring of Nestor and 
other large signet rings, and uses these comparisons to discuss workshop 
practices, stylistic development, and the circulation of iconographic themes. 
Davis also uses the Pylos evidence to refine the dating of Mycenaean elite 
burials and to interpret signet rings as markers of authority, identity, and 
religious ideology.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
