    {
      "local_ref": "d8",
      "document_summary": "The National Archaeological Museum of Greece's 
exhibition page \"Pylos of Nestor: A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed\" describes a 
temporary exhibition running from 26 May to 31 October 2026 in Athens, the third
stage of a traveling show that previously appeared at the Archaeological Museum 
of Messenia and the J. Paul Getty Museum. The exhibition, organized in 
collaboration with the Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia, the Getty Museum, 
and the University of Cincinnati's Department of Classics, presents 214 
antiquities from the Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia and 11 from the 
National Archaeological Museum alongside the museum's permanent Mycenaean 
collection. The narrative focuses on the development of power centers in 
Messenia leading to the establishment of the Palace at Ano Englianos, 
traditionally associated with Homeric Nestor, and uses burial monuments, 
prestigious objects such as rings and seals, and administrative records to 
illuminate Mycenaean social organization and ideology. The page emphasizes the 
