    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d9",
      "document_summary": "This History Blog post recounts the discovery and 
study of the so-called Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, 
focusing in particular on the Pylos Combat Agate, a finely carved sealstone. The
author describes how an intact Bronze Age grave near the palace was found to be 
the richest single grave yet discovered in Greece, containing ivory-handled 
gold-covered swords, four gold signet rings, numerous seal stones with Minoan 
motifs, beads, cups, and gold chains. After excavation, more than 3,000 burial 
objects underwent conservation at the Archaeological Museum of Chora. Among 
these was a small, lime-encrusted object later revealed to be the Pylos Combat 
Agate, a sealstone with an exceptionally detailed battle scene executed in 
miniature. The post cites University of Cincinnati reports stating that the 
agate's depiction of the human body rivals that of much later classical Greek 
art and may prompt reassessment of the development of Greek art and mythology. 
The narrative situates the Griffin Warrior tomb and its artifacts, including the
signet rings, in the broader popular fascination with Homeric heroes and the 
Palace of Nestor while cautioning that direct identification with a Homeric 
figure is chronologically unfounded.",
      "source_ref": "s9"
    }
