miniature relief scenes, which he labels \"the Ring of Nestor\" and treats as 
the centerpiece of the treasure.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "definition",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Focuses on formal characteristics and naming"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Based on summarized description, not direct quotation"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e3",
      "text": "Evans uses the combination of gold rings, bead-seals, bronze 
spear-heads, and a short sword of very late Mycenaean type from the Thisbê tomb 
to argue that the burial belongs to a late phase of the Mycenaean period.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Chronological inference from associated grave goods"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not specify absolute dates in the summary"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e4",
      "text": "Evans interprets the three-panel imagery on the Ring of Nestor’s 
bezel as depicting stages of a passage to the afterworld, drawing parallels with
Minoan religious motifs and arguing that the ring reflects Minoan beliefs 
adapted in a Mycenaean funerary context.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Iconographic interpretation by Evans"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Reconstructed from summary; not a verbatim quotation"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e5",
      "text": "The Heidelberg digital monograph outlines sections titled 
\"Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\"
and \"A glimpse into the Minoan after-world\" and includes plates I–V 
illustrating the Ring of Nestor and related objects.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "definition",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Describes structure and visual content of the monograph"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Specific plate captions are not reproduced"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e6",
      "text": "In the monograph, Evans compares the Ring of Nestor’s beaded band
and scene layout to other notable gold rings, including the so-called Ring of 
Minos, and suggests that similarities in band form and engraving technique 
indicate shared workshops or stylistic traditions.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Comparative stylistic argument"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Paraphrased from secondary summary"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e7",
      "text": "The HathiTrust catalog entry for \"The ring of Nestor\" records 
the title as including the subtitle \"a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and
a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\"
and notes that the work contains five plates.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "definition",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Standard catalog description"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Relies on catalog metadata rather than inspection of the volume"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e8",
      "text": "Jack L. Davis describes the Griffin Warrior grave as a Late 
Helladic IIA burial near Tholos Tomb IV close to the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, 
dating to approximately 1450–1400 BCE, and notes that it contained hundreds of 
artifacts, including four gold signet rings with Minoan-style ritual scenes.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Combines locational, chronological, and assemblage data"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Approximate dates; exact citation not provided in the summary"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "text": "In comparing Griffin Warrior finds to earlier discoveries, Davis 
refers to Evans’s Ring of Nestor and other large Aegean signet rings, using them
to discuss workshop traditions, stylistic evolution, and the spread of 
particular iconographic themes across Crete and the mainland.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Situates Ring of Nestor within broader scholarship on signet rings"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Summary does not reproduce the exact wording of Davis’s comparisons"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "text": "The Benedante blog characterizes the Ring of Nestor as a large 
gold signet ring from near Mycenaean Pylos with engraved scenes arranged in 
successive panels that form a complex narrative, and highlights the 
sophistication of the engraving and probable religious significance of the 
imagery.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "evidence_type": "definition",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Lay synthesis of form and iconography"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Non-academic blog; statements are secondary and interpretive"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "text": "The Benedante post compares the Ring of Nestor to the Ring of 
Minos and to rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb, arguing that these objects’ 
engraved scenes relate to conceptions of deities, rituals, and the afterlife in 
Minoan and Mycenaean religion.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Interpretive comparative statement"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Inference by a blog author; not a peer-reviewed analysis"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "text": "The Alexandria blog notes that ancient Aegean signet rings had 
finely engraved bezels with mythological and ritual scenes and cites the 
\"so-called Ring of Nestor — a large gold signet acquired near Pylos\" as an 
example of such elaborate ancient signet rings.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "evidence_type": "definition",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Statement illustrates modern popular recognition of the Ring of Nestor"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Commercial blog; does not provide primary references"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e13",
      "text": "The same Alexandria article comments that signet rings like the 
Ring of Nestor served both practical sealing functions and symbolic roles as 
markers of status and authority throughout the history of signet rings.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Broad functional generalization"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not present specific evidence for the Ring of Nestor’s use"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e14",
      "text": "Archaeology.wiki reports that the temporary exhibition \"Princes 
of Pylos. Bronze Age Treasures of Messenia\" at the Archaeological Museum of 
Messenia presents approximately 1,400 grave goods from the Griffin Warrior tomb 
and related Bronze Age contexts.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Quantitative information about exhibit contents"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Rounded figure; exact count not independently verified here"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e15",
      "text": "The Archaeology.wiki article states that the Griffin Warrior 
tomb, discovered in 2015 near Chamber Tomb IV about 150 meters northeast of the 
Palace of Nestor, yielded signet rings from the warrior’s grave and a golden 
ring from the Chamber Tomb of Antheia, as well as golden cups, weapons, and 
other luxury objects illustrating elite Mycenaean burial customs and exchange 
networks.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Combines discovery context with a list of notable exhibited items"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not identify any item as the Ring of Nestor"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e16",
      "text": "The article notes that the \"Princes of Pylos\" exhibition is 
organized by the Greek Ministry of Culture and the Ephorate of Antiquities of 
Messenia and that it highlights collaboration with Jack L. Davis and Sharon 
Stocker of the University of Cincinnati, underscoring institutional stewardship 
and research partnerships.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "evidence_type": "policy",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Describes organizational and collaborative framework of the exhibition"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e17",
      "text": "The National Archaeological Museum’s exhibition page states that 
\"Pylos of Nestor: A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed\" runs from 26 May to 31 October
2026 in Athens and that this exhibition follows earlier stages at the 
Archaeological Museum of Messenia and the J. Paul Getty Museum.",
      "document_ref": "d8",
      "source_ref": "s8",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Provides dates and sequence for the traveling exhibition"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e18",
      "text": "The same page reports that the exhibition features 214 
antiquities from the Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia and 11 from the 
National Archaeological Museum, displayed alongside the museum’s permanent 
Mycenaean collection, and that it traces the development of power centers 
culminating in the Palace at Ano Englianos, traditionally identified with 
Homeric Nestor.",
      "document_ref": "d8",
      "source_ref": "s8",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Provides object counts and thematic focus",
        "Uses the phrase \"traditionally associated\" regarding Nestor"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not list individual artifacts by name"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e19",
      "text": "The museum page explains that the exhibition integrates 
excavation diaries, drawings, and archival photographs into the display to 
demonstrate the archaeological research process and how knowledge of Mycenaean 
Pylos and its artifacts is constructed.",
      "document_ref": "d8",
      "source_ref": "s8",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Describes curatorial and educational methods"
      ],
      "limitations": [],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e20",
      "text": "The History Blog recounts that the Griffin Warrior tomb near the 
Palace of Nestor was an intact Bronze Age grave whose wealth made it the richest
single grave found in Greece to date, with contents including ivory-handled 
gold-covered swords, four gold signet rings, numerous seal stones with Minoan 
motifs, beads, cups, and gold chains.",
      "document_ref": "d9",
      "source_ref": "s9",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Reflects contemporary appraisal of the tomb’s richness"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Blog; phrasing may be hyperbolic or time-bound"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e21",
      "text": "The same blog states that after excavation, more than 3,000 
burial objects from the Griffin Warrior tomb were conserved and studied at the 
Archaeological Museum of Chora, and that a small lime-encrusted object among 
them was eventually revealed to be the Pylos Combat Agate, a sealstone with a 
highly detailed miniature battle scene.",
      "document_ref": "d9",
      "source_ref": "s9",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Describes conservation work and identification of a key artifact"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Object count and characterization drawn from a blog post"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e22",
      "text": "The History Blog notes that University of Cincinnati researchers 
described the Pylos Combat Agate’s depiction of the human body as rivaling 
classical Greek art and suggested that such workmanship may require a 
reassessment of the development of Greek art and mythology in the Bronze Age.",
      "document_ref": "d9",
      "source_ref": "s9",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": [
        "Summarizes interpretive claims attributed to academic researchers"
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Not a direct quote; derived from blog paraphrase"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "conflicts": [
    {
      "local_ref": "x1",
      "description": "There is a discrepancy regarding the provenance of the 
Ring of Nestor: Evans’s primary publications associate the Ring of Nestor with a
Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, whereas later secondary 
and popular sources such as the Benedante blog and the Alexandria jewelry blog 
describe the Ring of Nestor as a large gold signet ring discovered or acquired 
near Mycenaean Pylos.",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "e1",
        "e2",
        "e10",
        "e12"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
