AI\n\nThis paper presents an analysis of the iconography surrounding the 'Ring 
of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates. By 
revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, the paper aims to 
contribute to the ongoing discourse regarding the ring's significance in Minoan 
and Mycenaean art. Observations draw upon historical scholarship and recent 
discoveries to question and affirm previous conclusions related to the ring's 
motifs and their implications.\n\n## Key takeaways sparkles AI\n\n1. The text 
explores the authenticity of the 'Ring of Nestor' through iconographic 
analysis.\n2. Contradictions in technical analysis highlight the importance of 
iconography in Aegean studies.\n3. The connection between incurved altars and 
the ring's depiction supports its authenticity.\n4. The ring's iconography may 
reflect cultural practices from the 1920s.\n5. Generations of scholars have 
debated the genuineness of both Nestor's and Minos' rings.\n\n## Related 
papers\n\n[Towards a Consideration of Unprovenanced Ancient and Medieval Finger 
Rings](https://www.academia.edu/37745254/Towards_a_Consideration_of_Unprovenance
d_Ancient_and_Medieval_Finger_Rings)\n\n[TN 
Pollio](https://independent.academia.edu/TNPollio)\n\nThis article will 
hopefully incentivize researchers to consider examining the untoward thousands 
of unprovenanced base metal and silver alloy finger rings being uncovered at 
sites from the U.K to Eurasia. Documented artifacts from controlled and 
stratified environments are of course essential towards understanding ring 
typology and routes of transmission across temporal and spatial environments. 
However, these standard methodologies should not preclude consideration of 
random and under-documented finds from non-professional sources. The rationale 
is simply that, within the last several decades, a significant proportion of new
finds have in fact been made through amateur channels; and the rings they've 
uncovered may potentially contribute to our understanding of these underreported
artifacts. Within the last few decades, the proliferation of metal detecting and
use of the internet have ensured the issue is no longer of availability, but 
identity. The era of scarcity is obviously over, in view of the burgeoning array
of base metal rings encountered on the international market. Any a priori 
disqualification of such a vast resource of emerging dat…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
