what really intrigues scholars, says Stocker, is the “bigger world picture.” The
very first organized Greek society belonged to the Mycenaeans, whose kingdoms 
exploded out of nowhere on the Greek mainland around 1600 B.C. Although they 
disappeared equally dramatically a few hundred years later, giving way to 
several centuries known as the Greek Dark Ages, before the rise of “classical” 
Greece, the Mycenaeans sowed the seeds of our common traditions, including art 
and architecture, language, philosophy and literature, even democracy and 
