      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "A. G. Vlachopoulos’ chapter focuses on the Ring of 
Nestor as an Aegean signet ring and examines its authenticity and iconography. 
It highlights that the ring’s engraved scene is exceptionally complex, with 
fourteen human figures and several fantastic creatures, making it the most 
densely populated multi-figure composition among known Aegean signet rings. The 
study analyses the ring face as organized in multiple registers or zones, each 
containing combinations of human participants, hybrid or monstrous beings, and 
ritual settings. Vlachopoulos discusses specific compositional groupings such as
processional or ritual scenes, the presence of divine or epiphanic figures, and 
the interaction of humans with altars or other cult equipment. The chapter 
situates these motifs within broader Aegean glyptic iconography and debates 
about whether the ring is an authentic Late Bronze Age product or a modern 
forgery, using detailed comparison with other seal images and stylistic 
criteria.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Theodore Eliopoulos’ article offers a focused 
re-examination of the iconography of the Ring of Nestor within the context of 
Aegean forgery debates. It analyses specific visual elements on the ring, 
including human figures in ritual postures, composite or fantastic creatures, 
sacred architecture, and cult objects. A key point is the identification of 
incurved altars depicted on the ring; Eliopoulos argues that the way these 
altars are rendered and associated with other elements supports the ring’s 
authenticity because similar altars are attested in genuine Aegean material. The
paper systematically compares the ring’s scenes with other Minoan and Mycenaean 
glyptic and wall-painting motifs, examining figure gestures, spatial 
arrangement, and the relationship between worshippers, deities or epiphanic 
figures, monsters, and built cult features. Through these iconographic 
observations, Eliopoulos reassesses where the ring fits within Bronze Age Aegean
religious imagery and the forgery debate.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The article in the Journal of Ancient Egyptian 
Interconnections discusses the so‑called Nestor Ring as a \"pseudo‑Minoan\" 
object and analyzes its iconography in relation to Egyptian visual traditions. 
It notes that the ring was found in 1924 and that Sir Arthur Evans accepted it 
as authentic while many later scholars considered it a forgery. The study 
compares details of the ring’s scene—including human and divine or semi‑divine 
figures, ritual arrangements, and possible Nile or Egyptian motifs—with Egyptian
iconographic conventions, arguing that aspects of its composition may derive 
from or imitate Egyptian models. The paper uses these comparisons to question or
refine its cultural attribution, exploring whether the ring’s imagery reflects 
genuine Aegean religious iconography, Egyptian influence, or modern pastiche, 
and addresses how the Egyptian parallels affect assessments of the ring’s 
authenticity and date.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’ 1925 monograph introduces and 
describes the Ring of Nestor among a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings 
and bead seals from Thisbê in Boeotia. Evans provides a narrative interpretation
of the ring’s engraved scene as a glimpse into a Minoan after‑world, linking its
motifs to funerary and otherworldly beliefs. He describes the arrangement of 
multiple human figures and divine or supernatural beings, their actions and 
attributes, the presence of altars or architectural structures, and various 
monstrous or composite creatures. The text sets out the layout of the ring face 
in distinct scenes or registers and connects these elements to a coherent 
religious or eschatological interpretation. Evans also discusses the discovery 
context, the association with other gold objects, and uses stylistic and 
iconographic arguments to support an early date and Minoan affiliation for the 
ring.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The Cambridge University Press chapter on Aegean art 
in the final palatial period of Knossos cites the Ring of Nestor (catalogued as 
CMS VI, no. 277) as an example of an extraordinary religious scene on a signet 
ring. It notes the ring’s remarkable decor and characterizes its imagery as a 
complex cultic or ritual composition, likely involving multiple participants and
sacred symbols. The chapter provides catalog-style information, including the 
CMS reference and its classification as a ring bearing a religious scene, and 
briefly situates the ring within the corpus of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic as an 
unusually elaborate representation of ritual activity.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    },
    {
