### Source 1

- **Title**: (2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in
Aegean Iconography*, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223–252.
- **URL**: 
https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_
the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspect
ives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252
- **Publisher / Author**: Academia.edu hosting a paper by **Andreas G. 
Vlachopoulos**.
- **Date**: 2020 (paper); upload date not clearly stated in snippet.
- **Source type**: Scholarly article (journal volume chapter; hosted on 
Academia.edu).
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" Evans Davis Archaeology.wiki 
National Archaeological Museum -site:en.wikipedia.org`
- **Raw / faithful summary**:
  - The abstract states that the paper presents many years of research on the 
so‑called **Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277)**, documenting its 
**authenticity**.
  - Vlachopoulos reassesses prior tendencies (especially in the early 20th 
century) to regard certain **Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings**—including the Ring of 
Nestor and the Ring of Minos—as **fakes**. He argues this tendency has now been 
**revised in favour of their authenticity**.
  - The revision is supported by:
    - Recent finds of **iconographic subjects of comparable resonance** (wall 
paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories, etc.) from the **first centuries of 
the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC)**, and
    - More thorough study of the **techniques and craftsmanship** of these 
unique signet rings.
  - A key focus is the main pictorial unit of the Ring of Nestor: Evans’s 
so‑called “**watch‑dog**” or “**dragon**”, the guardian of an old “tree of the 
world”. Vlachopoulos argues this creature is better identified as a 
**scorpion**, interpreted as a major **apotropaic symbol** in Bronze Age Aegean 
iconography.
  - The “key takeaways” section (apparently added by Academia’s AI) summarizes, 
among other points:
    - The Ring of Nestor **measures 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm**, making it the **fifth 
largest Aegean signet ring**.
    - Research **confirms its authenticity**, contrary to previous arguments 
(1920s) that it was a forgery.
    - The **scorpion motif** plays a crucial role in interpreting the ring’s 
iconography.
    - The ring belongs to the **Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC)** and 
contributes to understanding Aegean glyptic art.
- **Relevance to your audit goal**:
  - Provides **precise physical dimensions** and relative size ranking among 
Aegean signet rings.
  - Gives **chronological attribution** (16th–15th century BC Late Bronze Age).
  - Provides **iconographic interpretation** (scorpion vs. dragon/watch‑dog, 
tree of the world).
  - Directly addresses **authenticity debates** and the history of scholarly 
skepticism.
  - Suitable behind citations that support physical description, dating, 
iconography, and authenticity claims.
- **Compliance checks**:
  - **Domain**: academia.edu (not Wikipedia).
  - **URL**: not `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring_of_Nestor`.
  - **Source type**: scholarly article, not social media / forum / UGC 
aggregator.

