### Source 2

- **Title**: This 3,500‑Year‑Old Greek Tomb Upended What We Thought We Knew 
About the Roots of Western Civilization
- **URL**: 
https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/golden-warrior-greek-tomb-exposes-roots-w
estern-civilization-180961441
- **Publisher / Author**: Smithsonian Magazine; article by **Jo Marchant**.
- **Date**: Smithsonian feature from 2017 (exact date in full article; host 
snippet shows context but not the specific day).
- **Source type**: Magazine / popular science / news feature.
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" Evans Davis Archaeology.wiki 
National Archaeological Museum -site:en.wikipedia.org`
- **Raw / faithful summary**:
  - The article reports on the discovery of the **“Griffin Warrior” tomb** near 
the **Palace of Nestor at Pylos**, southwest coast of Greece, excavated starting
in 2015.
  - Archaeologists **Jack Davis** and **Sharon Stocker** (University of 
Cincinnati) led the excavation.
  - The tomb contained a wealth of artifacts: bronze basins, weapons, gold and 
silver cups, beads of carnelian, amethyst, amber and gold, stone seals, and 
**four stunning gold rings**.
  - It emphasizes the significance of Mycenaean civilization (ca. 1600–1100 BC) 
in the roots of “Western civilization,” and situates the **Palace of Nestor** 
within the Mycenaean context.
  - While the piece focuses on the Griffin Warrior and its rings, it provides 
background on **Pylos**, **Nestor**, and the Mycenaean period more generally.
- **Relevance to your audit goal**:
  - Contextualizes **Pylos and the Palace of Nestor** as an important Bronze Age
site.
  - Useful as a **background source** for Mycenaean Pylos and the association 
with Nestor in later tradition, though it does **not directly discuss the Ring 
of Nestor (CMS VI, 277)**.
  - Could be used to support general statements about the **archaeological 
setting at Pylos**, Mycenaean tombs with gold rings, and modern excavations by 
Davis and Stocker.
- **Compliance checks**:
  - **Domain**: smithsonianmag.com (not Wikipedia).
  - **URL**: not blocked.
  - **Source type**: Magazine article, not social media / forum / UGC 
aggregator.

