made of carnelian, amethyst, amber, and gold, intricately carved stone seals, 
and four gold rings.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["object_types: metal_vessels_weapons_jewelry_seals_rings"],
      "limitations": ["the description is selective and may omit additional 
minor items from the assemblage"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "text": "Marchant explains that the Griffin Warrior tomb and the Palace of
Nestor belong to the Mycenaean civilization, which she dates broadly from about 
1600 to 1100 BC, and she links these finds to broader questions about the 
origins of Western civilization.",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["chronology: ca_1600-1100_BC", "civilization: Mycenaean"],
      "limitations": ["chronological range is framed for a general audience and 
not presented as a technical periodization scheme"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "text": "The ARCH347 syllabus outlines that the course \"When Egypt meets 
the Aegean\" explores cultural, economic, and artistic interactions between 
Egypt and the Aegean during the Bronze Age, emphasizing the Late Bronze Age 
period.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["course_focus: Egypt-Aegean_interconnections", "period: 
Late_Bronze_Age"],
      "limitations": ["detailed weekly topics are not provided in the summarized
material"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "text": "Within its list of online resources, the ARCH347 syllabus directs
students to the Latsis Foundation’s Museums Cycle, the Dartmouth Aegean 
Prehistoric Archaeology website, the Nestor Aegean Bibliography, and the 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens, among other institutional sites.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["resource_types: 
museum_publications_university_sites_bibliography"],
      "limitations": ["exact URLs and resource descriptions are not fully 
visible in the summary"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "text": "The January 2020 Nestor Bibliography issue is described as an 
annual publication of the Department of Classics at the University of Cincinnati
that compiles recent references on Aegean prehistory and related areas using 
standardized abbreviations and thematic sections.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["publisher: 
University_of_Cincinnati_Department_of_Classics"],
      "limitations": ["specific section headings and pagination are not 
mentioned in the summary"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e13",
      "text": "The Nestor Bibliography includes not only bibliographic listings 
but also announcements of conferences, grants, and calls for papers relevant to 
Aegean archaeology.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["content_type: announcements_plus_bibliography"],
      "limitations": ["frequency and detail of such announcements are not 
specified"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e14",
      "text": "The Latsis Foundation’s \"Mycenae\" monograph discusses the 
Bronze Age citadel, including its fortifications, palace complex, cult center, 
and cemeteries, and surveys Mycenaean art forms such as frescoes, metalwork, 
stone vessels, ivories, terracotta figurines, and pottery.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["site: Mycenae", "period: Bronze_Age_Mycenaean"],
      "limitations": ["treatment of specific individual rings or seals is not 
described in the summary"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e15",
      "text": "The monograph notes that the Archaeological Museum of Mycenae and
the National Archaeological Museum hold and display many of the artifacts from 
Mycenae under the authority of the Hellenic Ministry of Culture and Sports.",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["institutions: 
Archaeological_Museum_of_Mycenae_National_Archaeological_Museum"],
      "limitations": ["no specific inventory numbers or artifact names are given
in the summarized description"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e16",
      "text": "The UC Press monograph on Mycenaean Pylos describes how 
archaeologists such as Carl Blegen and Konstantinos Kourouniotis explored the 
region and identified the Mycenaean palace complex now known as the Palace of 
Nestor.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["site: Pylos", "palace: Palace_of_Nestor"],
      "limitations": ["the summary does not specify dates or exact excavation 
seasons for this work"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e17",
      "text": "The same monograph situates the Palace of Nestor within a 
narrative of state formation, arguing that Mycenaean Pylos developed into an 
organized palatial state with complex administrative systems and architectural 
planning.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["interpretive_focus: state_formation"],
      "limitations": ["specific administrative features are not detailed in the 
summarized description"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e18",
      "text": "The Dartmouth Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology Lesson 24 
bibliography is described as including sections on general Mycenaean art, 
iconography and color, frescoes, pottery production and technology, and seals 
and signet rings within the broader topic of Mycenaean pictorial art and 
pottery.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["topic: Mycenaean_pictorial_art_and_pottery"],
      "limitations": ["individual works listed under the seals and signet rings 
section are not named in the summary"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e19",
      "text": "According to the description, the Lesson 24 bibliography cites 
symposia volumes, festschriften, and key monographs that are standard references
for the study of Aegean Bronze Age art and glyptic.",
      "document_ref": "d7",
      "source_ref": "s7",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["literature_types: symposia_festschriften_monographs"],
      "limitations": ["no example titles are quoted in the summary"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e20",
      "text": "The \"Fakes and Dubitanda\" chapter in \"The Art and Archaeology 
of the Aegean Bronze Age\" is introduced as dealing with suspect or disputed 
objects in Aegean Bronze Age art, including rings and other artifacts whose 
authenticity has been questioned.",
      "document_ref": "d8",
      "source_ref": "s8",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["topic: disputed_Aegean_Bronze_Age_objects"],
      "limitations": ["the summary does not explicitly list the specific objects
or rings treated in the chapter"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e21",
      "text": "The chapter is described as situating debates over fakes and 
dubitanda within the overall structure of the book, which covers different 
phases of Aegean prehistory and examines how authenticity issues intersect with 
broader interpretive questions about Aegean art.",
      "document_ref": "d8",
      "source_ref": "s8",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["focus: historiography_and_method"],
