"20260628152251657830_e85ded99", "text": "Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph 
presents and interprets a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals discovered in association with a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near Thisbê 
(Thisbe) in Boeotia, Greece. It is divided into a first part describing the 
discovery context, composition, and physical features of the treasure, and a 
second part using the engraved imagery to argue for Minoan conceptions of the 
afterlife. Within the treasure Evans singles out one elaborate gold signet ring,
designating it the “ring of Nestor” and associating it with the Homeric figure 
Nestor. He describes its beaded gold hoop and complex engraved scenes, linking 
their iconography to Minoan religious and funerary ideas and to broader 
Minoan-Mycenaean and Egyptian artistic connections. The plates illustrate the 
rings and bead-seals, including the Nestor ring, and the text situates the 
treasure chronologically within Mycenaean and Minoan phases."}, {"id": 
