"20260628152251729228_5d864f18", "text": "Evans’s 1936 lecture \"The Minoan 
World,\" republished by the Aegeus Society, discusses the development and 
discovery of Minoan civilization and its connections with Mycenaean Greece and 
Egypt. Among various examples of Aegean art Evans refers to signet rings and 
bead-seals from Boeotia, including a later group at Thisbê, whose motifs such as
a lion-spearing scene and spear-shaft are compared with Mycenaean and other 
Boeotian gold objects. He mentions a gold bead acquired in 1910 from a Boeotian 
peasant and later obtained for the Ashmolean Museum, comparing it with the 
Thisbê series. The lecture treats the Thisbê treasure, consistent with his 
earlier study, as important evidence for early Aegean art and for cultural 
interconnections, though it does not repeat the full \"Ring of Nestor\" title in
the cited passage."}, {"id": "20260628152251782676_93905c8c", "type": "Source", 
"source_type": "paper", "title": "The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian
Iconography", "url": 
