g_and_Its_Egyptian_Iconography"}, {"id": "20260628152555188364_4c812536", 
"type": "Analysis", "analysis_type": "insight", "text": "Evidence collected so 
far shows that the Ring of Nestor is a large gold signet ring, usually dated to 
the Late Minoan IB–II period, reportedly found in a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near
Thisbê/Thísvi in Boeotia as part of a group of gold signet rings and bead-seals 
acquired by Arthur Evans and later associated with the Ashmolean Museum. 
Scholarly and catalogue descriptions emphasize its unusually large size, beaded 
hoop, and complex multi-scene engraving, and it is frequently cited in 
discussions of Aegean glyptic, Minoan concepts of the afterlife, and debates 
over pseudo-Minoan or possibly forged artifacts. To complete the article, we 
need to synthesize detailed iconographic descriptions, specific interpretive 
arguments (including any links to Homeric Nestor), and clearly laid-out debates 
on authenticity and attribution, while designing an article body whose 
paragraphs all carry bracketed numeric citations and a references section 
mapping cleanly to non-Wikipedia URLs."}, {"id": 
