Knossos cites the Ring of Nestor (catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277) as an example 
of an extraordinary religious scene on a signet ring. It notes the ring’s 
remarkable decor and characterizes its imagery as a complex cultic or ritual 
composition, likely involving multiple participants and sacred symbols. The 
chapter provides catalog-style information, including the CMS reference and its 
classification as a ring bearing a religious scene, and briefly situates the 
ring within the corpus of Aegean Bronze Age glyptic as an unusually elaborate 
representation of ritual activity."}, {"id": "20260628152757678865_f91775e7", 
