"20260628152756486280_e10b184b", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260628152124716216_d7950336", "text": "Theodore Eliopoulos’ article offers a 
focused re-examination of the iconography of the Ring of Nestor within the 
context of Aegean forgery debates. It analyses specific visual elements on the 
ring, including human figures in ritual postures, composite or fantastic 
creatures, sacred architecture, and cult objects. A key point is the 
identification of incurved altars depicted on the ring; Eliopoulos argues that 
the way these altars are rendered and associated with other elements supports 
the ring’s authenticity because similar altars are attested in genuine Aegean 
material. The paper systematically compares the ring’s scenes with other Minoan 
and Mycenaean glyptic and wall-painting motifs, examining figure gestures, 
spatial arrangement, and the relationship between worshippers, deities or 
epiphanic figures, monsters, and built cult features. Through these iconographic
observations, Eliopoulos reassesses where the ring fits within Bronze Age Aegean
religious imagery and the forgery debate."}, {"id": 
"20260628152756871363_fdb2f9a1", "type": "Statement", "confidence": "medium", 
"qualifiers": ["assessment based on specific iconographic motif", "argument 
within forgery debate"], "source_id": "20260628152124716216_d7950336", "text": 
"Eliopoulos argues that the depiction of incurved altars on the Ring of Nestor, 
and their correspondence with other Aegean examples, supports the authenticity 
of the ring.", "statement_key": "statement:a7d031ffa04db2649863aea6", 
"document_id": "20260628152756486280_e10b184b", "limitations": ["restated from 
summarized description of the article rather than explicit quoted passage"]}, 
