"20260628152756515880_e0eb19d5", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260628152756345344_1e41be57", "text": "The article in the Journal of Ancient 
Egyptian Interconnections discusses the so‑called Nestor Ring as a 
\"pseudo‑Minoan\" object and analyzes its iconography in relation to Egyptian 
visual traditions. It notes that the ring was found in 1924 and that Sir Arthur 
Evans accepted it as authentic while many later scholars considered it a 
forgery. The study compares details of the ring’s scene—including human and 
divine or semi‑divine figures, ritual arrangements, and possible Nile or 
Egyptian motifs—with Egyptian iconographic conventions, arguing that aspects of 
its composition may derive from or imitate Egyptian models. The paper uses these
comparisons to question or refine its cultural attribution, exploring whether 
the ring’s imagery reflects genuine Aegean religious iconography, Egyptian 
influence, or modern pastiche, and addresses how the Egyptian parallels affect 
assessments of the ring’s authenticity and date."}, {"id": 
