"20260628152756457504_37e153bf", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260628152124654716_343e1a7d", "text": "A. G. Vlachopoulos’ chapter focuses on
the Ring of Nestor as an Aegean signet ring and examines its authenticity and 
iconography. It highlights that the ring’s engraved scene is exceptionally 
complex, with fourteen human figures and several fantastic creatures, making it 
the most densely populated multi-figure composition among known Aegean signet 
rings. The study analyses the ring face as organized in multiple registers or 
zones, each containing combinations of human participants, hybrid or monstrous 
beings, and ritual settings. Vlachopoulos discusses specific compositional 
groupings such as processional or ritual scenes, the presence of divine or 
epiphanic figures, and the interaction of humans with altars or other cult 
equipment. The chapter situates these motifs within broader Aegean glyptic 
iconography and debates about whether the ring is an authentic Late Bronze Age 
product or a modern forgery, using detailed comparison with other seal images 
and stylistic criteria."}, {"id": "20260628152948338209_03ee72ac", "type": 
