"source_id": "20260628152251680187_d268dd55", "text": "The Heidelberg digital 
page for Arthur Evans’ \"The ring of Nestor\" is an interface for accessing the 
scanned monograph. It primarily contains technical instructions on using the 
viewer, such as rotating images, zoom levels, and navigation controls. The page 
confirms that the Evans text and plates are available for online consultation 
and that the ring’s original description and illustrations can be reached by 
scrolling to the relevant pages, but it does not itself describe the ring’s 
iconography."}, {"id": "20260628152756551465_fdf11c13", "type": "Document", 
"source_id": "20260628152407087140_b91ef2dd", "text": "Arthur J. Evans’ 1925 
monograph introduces and describes the Ring of Nestor among a sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê in Boeotia. Evans 
provides a narrative interpretation of the ring’s engraved scene as a glimpse 
into a Minoan after‑world, linking its motifs to funerary and otherworldly 
beliefs. He describes the arrangement of multiple human figures and divine or 
supernatural beings, their actions and attributes, the presence of altars or 
architectural structures, and various monstrous or composite creatures. The text
sets out the layout of the ring face in distinct scenes or registers and 
connects these elements to a coherent religious or eschatological 
interpretation. Evans also discusses the discovery context, the association with
other gold objects, and uses stylistic and iconographic arguments to support an 
early date and Minoan affiliation for the ring."}, {"id": 
"20260628152758039722_9a07a5e7", "type": "Evidence", "evidence_type": "other", 
"confidence": "high", "claim": "The Heidelberg page lists viewer controls such 
as \"Bild im Uhrzeigersinn um 90° drehen…\" and \"Zoomstufe 100% [1] • 160% 
[2]…\", indicating that the Evans 1925 monograph pages can be navigated and 
viewed online.", "qualifiers": ["evidence of digital accessibility of the 
monograph"], "source_id": "20260628152251680187_d268dd55", "text": "The 
