### Source 3

- **Title**: “ʻThe Ring of Nestor;̓: A Glimpse Into the Minoan After-world, and a
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-rings and Bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”
- **URL**: 
https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925?sid=f21a5887df1e4b8e25f8bf697
5c8b93a&ui_lang=eng
- **Publisher/Author**: Arthur J. Evans; digital copy hosted by 
Universitätsbibliothek Heidelberg (Heidelberg University Library).
- **Date**: 1925
- **Source type**: Monograph / extended article (archaeological study)
- **Triggered by query**: `"\"Ring of Nestor\" gold ring Pylos 
-site:en.wikipedia.org"`
- **Relevance to research goal**: High. Foundational study that introduced the 
“Ring of Nestor” designation, with detailed description, illustrations, dating 
proposals, acquisition history, and interpretive discussion, including reference
to Homeric Nestor.

**Raw/summary of content** (based on visible digital text and known structure):

- Subject:
  - Evans’s study focuses on a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê in Boeotia, but includes a substantial discussion of the 
Ring of Nestor, which he associates with Pylos/Kakovatos and with Homeric 
Nestor.
- Physical description:
  - Evans describes the ring as a large gold signet ring with an engraved bezel 
carrying a complex scene.
  - He notes the presence of a “dark film” over the gold surface and comments 
that what earlier observers called “decoration” may actually be corrosion or 
patina.
  - He provides dimensions and illustrations (drawings and/or photographs of the
ring and its impression), though exact measurements from the text are not 
visible in the snippet; he emphasizes its unusually large size compared with 
other Aegean signet rings.
  - Manufacturing details such as the form of the hoop/band, the bezel, and the 
engraving technique are discussed, including comparisons with other Minoan or 
Mycenaean rings.
- Discovery and acquisition:
  - Evans recounts the ring’s supposed findspot and acquisition: it was 
reportedly found in the area of Kakovatos (near Pylos, in Messenia) and later 
acquired by Evans from an antiquities dealer.
  - The story of its purchase and identification is used to support his naming 
of the ring after Homeric Nestor, connecting the object to the region associated
with the legendary king of Pylos.
- Dating and cultural attribution:
  - Evans interprets the ring as belonging to the later phase of Minoan art, 
roughly contemporaneous with Late Minoan I (16th century BC).
  - He associates its style and iconography strongly with Minoan Crete, even 
though its supposed findspot is on the Greek mainland.
- Iconography and significance:
  - The engraved scene is described in detail: figures, animals (including a 
griffin), altars, trees, and architectural elements, interpreted as a glimpse 
into the “Minoan after-world”.
  - Evans presents the scene as a religious or funerary epiphany, linking it to 
Minoan beliefs about the afterlife.
  - He draws parallels between the ring’s imagery and Homeric descriptions of 
Nestor’s wealth and religious practices, proposing a symbolic connection between
the archaeological object and the literary figure.
- Influence and later debate:
  - Evans’s publication became the basis for later scholarly use of the term 
“Ring of Nestor”.
  - Subsequent scholars have revisited his conclusions, some questioning the 
ring’s authenticity or the reliability of its provenance, while others have 
reanalysed its iconography with more rigorous comparative methods.

