    {"local_ref": "d1", "document_summary": "Chapter on Minoan glyptic in a 
Cambridge University Press volume includes a catalogue-style entry for the Ring 
of Nestor, a gold signet ring reportedly from Kakovatos in Messenia. It records 
the ring’s basic physical characteristics, such as its length (approximately 
3.27 cm), and notes its large size in comparison with other Aegean rings. The 
entry assigns the ring to the Late Minoan IB–II period (around 1530–1400 BC) and
characterizes it as part of the Minoan glyptic tradition, despite its mainland 
findspot. The Ashmolean Museum in Oxford is given as the current repository, 
