work by I. Pini and a 1998 article in the Oxford Journal of Archaeology, as part
of the ring’s bibliographic record.", "source_ref": "s1"},
    {"local_ref": "d2", "document_summary": "Vlachopoulos’s 2020 chapter is a 
dedicated study of the Ring of Nestor that focuses on questions of authenticity,
physical and stylistic analysis, and cultural attribution. It describes the ring
as a Late Bronze Age gold signet ring housed in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford 
and notes that it is among the largest known Aegean signet rings, giving 
dimensions of about 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and calling it the fifth largest Aegean 
signet. The study discusses details of the ring’s manufacture and surface, 
including mention of a dark film or patina previously interpreted as decorative.
It analyses the complex scene engraved on the bezel, including human figures, a 
central female figure, a griffin, architectural elements, altars, and trees, 
interpreting the composition as a ritual or epiphany scene characteristic of 
Minoan religious iconography. The chapter reviews earlier doubts about the 
ring’s authenticity, including stylistic and provenance-based objections, and 
compares it closely with securely provenanced rings. Vlachopoulos concludes that
current evidence supports the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor. He dates the 
ring to the Late Minoan I–II period (16th–15th century BC) and emphasizes its 
Minoan stylistic connections, even though it was reportedly found on the Greek 
mainland. The study also discusses previous attempts to link the ring to Homeric
Nestor but ultimately prioritizes an interpretation grounded in Aegean 
iconography.", "source_ref": "s2"},
    {"local_ref": "d3", "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 study 
presents a detailed account of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê in Boeotia and includes a major section on the Ring of 
Nestor. Evans describes the Ring of Nestor as a large gold signet ring with an 
elaborately engraved bezel and comments on a dark film over its gold surface, 
which earlier observers considered decorative. He emphasizes the ring’s 
exceptional size compared with other Aegean signet rings and provides 
measurements and illustrations of both the ring and its impressions. Evans 
recounts the reported discovery of the ring in the area of Kakovatos near Pylos 
in Messenia and its acquisition from an antiquities dealer, using this 
provenance to associate the ring with Pylos and the legendary Homeric figure 
Nestor. He dates the ring to a later phase of Minoan art, corresponding roughly 
to Late Minoan I, and attributes its style and iconography firmly to the Minoan 
tradition, despite the mainland context. Evans offers an extensive description 
of the bezel scene, identifying figures, a griffin, altars, trees, and 
architectural elements, and interprets the composition as a depiction of the 
Minoan afterlife and religious epiphany. He connects the imagery with Homeric 
descriptions of Nestor’s wealth and piety, proposing a symbolic link between the
object and the literary king of Pylos. The publication became foundational for 
subsequent use of the term 'Ring of Nestor' and for later debates about its 
authenticity, provenance, and iconography.", "source_ref": "s3"},
    {"local_ref": "d4", "document_summary": "Evans’s article in the Journal of 
