{"query": {"id": "20260628152533258757_8bbc59f0", "handle": "q3.1", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" article_body_and_paragraph_citations full current article 
text headings paragraphs bracketed numeric citations verification of every 
factual claim cited 2024-2026", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": 
"20260628152844137825_5618303e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
metadata for Jack L. Davis's article notes that in May 2015 a large stone-built 
tomb of Late Helladic IIA date was discovered near Tholos Tomb IV at the Palace 
of Nestor, Pylos, and that the grave contained hundreds of artifacts and four 
gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes buried with a single adult
male nicknamed the 'Griffin Warrior'.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152407111081_271f8a03"], "citations": [17]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844211521_353b95a6", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
page summarizing 'The Lord of the Gold Rings' indicates that the Griffin Warrior
grave is dated to around 1450–1400 BCE based on pottery and associated grave 
goods and is assigned to the Late Helladic IIA period.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152407111081_271f8a03"], "citations": [17]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844291843_fad0534b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Heidelberg 
University Library digitization entry lists the full title of Evans's work as 
'The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia' and provides 
a DOI (https://doi.org/10.11588/diglit.808) along with links to PDFs containing 
Part I 'Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia' and Part II 'A glimpse into the Minoan after-world'.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152251657830_e85ded99"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844361989_0d01ffaa", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Heidelberg 
page associates keywords such as 'Nestor', 'Ring', 'Minoan culture', 'Thisvi', 
and 'Kakóvatos' with Evans's 1925 publication, indicating that the work 
addresses Minoan cultural context and the specific find location at Thisbê 
(Thisvi), Boeotia.", "source_ids": ["20260628152251657830_e85ded99"], 
"citations": [9]}, {"id": "20260628152844436171_b3cef614", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The HathiTrust catalog record for Record 000609034 lists the title 'The
ring of Nestor; a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure
of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia' and names 'Evans, 
Arthur, Sir, 1851–1941' as the author.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152407087140_b91ef2dd"], "citations": [16]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844501374_0eefffd8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
abstract for 'The \"Ring of Nestor\"' explains that the article presents new 
technical observations which, according to the author, support the authenticity 
of the Ring of Nestor and counter earlier suspicions that it might be a 
forgery.", "source_ids": ["20260628152841748020_95f45291"], "citations": [28]}, 
{"id": "20260628152844570988_b844bf57", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In 
summarizing the Ring of Nestor's museum history, the ResearchGate description 
notes that 'in 1995 it was once again placed on display', implying that the ring
had previously been removed from public exhibition.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152841748020_95f45291"], "citations": [28]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844642644_15545fc3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Within 
'Deconstructing a Narrative of Epiphany on the Isopata Ring', the author states 
that 'The ring is in the Athens National Museum, no. 1801. See CMS I, no. 219.' 
in reference to the Ring of Nestor.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152841779654_65a317ad"], "citations": [29]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844707414_e335f748", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Cambridge 
University Press online description of the chapter 'Fakes and Dubitanda' notes 
that while the Ring of Nestor is treated there as an authentic object, the 
author observes that 'doubts surrounding the \"Ring of Nestor\" have not ceased 
and probably never will.'", "source_ids": ["20260628152841807143_6101ab94"], 
"citations": [30]}, {"id": "20260628152844777716_d00f0cc8", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The chapter summary frames 'Fakes and Dubitanda' as an examination of 
questionable Aegean Bronze Age objects, underlining that the Ring of Nestor is 
included among artifacts whose authenticity and status have been debated within 
the field.", "source_ids": ["20260628152841807143_6101ab94"], "citations": 
[30]}, {"id": "20260628152844849080_8eb597b3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "A 
snippet from 'Traces of Places: Sacred Sites in Miniature on Minoan Gold Rings' 
notes that in the scene on the Ring of Nestor 'the ring of Nestor serves as a 
seat for a griffin', indicating that a throne or seat supports a griffin figure 
as part of the ring's iconography.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152841840115_dc815f56"], "citations": [31]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844915411_60ab6585", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 'Traces of 
Places' article situates the Ring of Nestor alongside other Minoan gold rings 
with complex sacred imagery and discusses their dating and style, referring to a
'gold Minoan-style engraved signet ring from Pylos' in the same context.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628152841840115_dc815f56"], "citations": [31]}, {"id": 
"20260628152844993862_cc5115a4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Past magazine 
article remarks that, prior to the Griffin Warrior discoveries, 'the only other'
gold signet ring known from the Pylos area was 'the famous Ring of 
Nestor–purchased by Arthur Evans', establishing a comparison between the new 
finds and the Ring of Nestor and mentioning Evans's acquisition of the latter.",
"source_ids": ["20260628152251706798_280e6a9f"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628152845076662_ad5eb6bf", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Past feature 
explains that the Griffin Warrior grave lay near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos 
and that its gold signet rings, richly decorated in a Minoan style, demonstrate 
close connections between the Mycenaean elite at Pylos and Minoan culture during
the Late Helladic IIA period.", "source_ids": ["20260628152251706798_280e6a9f"],
"citations": [11]}, {"id": "20260628152845141878_2e4b44be", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The National Archaeological Museum's 'The separation of the lovers' 
page describes a gold signet ring from the Tiryns Hoard (inv. no P 6209) as 
having a hoop diameter of 1.9 cm and a bezel measuring 2 by 3.4 cm, made of gold
and engraved with a scene showing two couples, likely depicting either a 
mythological story or a farewell before battle.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152841890562_bfbc98ca"], "citations": [32]}, {"id": 
"20260628152845209838_57c10460", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The same museum 
text notes that women in the scene wear long, pleated skirts and men wear 
Minoan-style loincloths and carry swords and spears, and it interprets the 
imagery as evidence that emotions such as love could be expressed in Mycenaean 
art.", "source_ids": ["20260628152841890562_bfbc98ca"], "citations": [32]}, 
{"id": "20260628152845268382_1129c365", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
bibliography on 'The separation of the lovers' page cites A. Sakellariou, 'Die 
minoischen und mykenischen Siegel des Nationalmuseums in Athen' (1964) and 'CMS 
I, 180', demonstrating that National Archaeological Museum seals and rings are 
cross-referenced with Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel catalog 
numbers.", "source_ids": ["20260628152841890562_bfbc98ca"], "citations": [32]}, 
{"id": "20260628152845335140_840e8ab6", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The History
Blog post on the Griffin Warrior grave notes that archaeologists discovered an 
exceptional assemblage of grave goods near the Palace of Nestor, including gold 
signet rings and sealstones, and describes the 'Pylos Combat Agate' as a small, 
previously encrusted sealstone whose cleaned surface revealed a finely detailed 
scene of a warrior defeating an opponent.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152407230397_2a364cb6"], "citations": [22]}, {"id": 
"20260628152845392712_cf4504e0", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The blog author 
asserts that the anatomical precision and dynamism of the Pylos Combat Agate's 
engraving are so advanced that similar quality is not seen again in Greek art 
until a thousand years later during the Classical period, suggesting that this 
find may 'rewrite the history of ancient Greek art'.", "source_ids": 
["20260628152407230397_2a364cb6"], "citations": [22]}, {"id": 
"20260628152845453903_40277b33", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Joslyn Art 
Museum event description for 'The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of
Pylos' explains that Jack L. Davis and Sharon R. Stocker will present their 
excavations at the Palace of Nestor, including the Griffin Warrior tomb and two 
Mycenaean tholos tombs, and that these discoveries illuminate the rise of 
Mycenaean civilization and the formation of early European states.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628152841947872_ef9c2316"], "citations": [33]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 20, "total": 32, "next_offset": 20}
