submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': '', 
'section_type': 'lead', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628152126753218_5108bfb5', '20260628154550905025_daabb80d', 
'20260628154550986366_5384281c', '20260628154551096189_a8a9cb09', 
'20260628154551214211_e5aa16ec', '20260628153403719533_748a1f3a'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is a gold signet ring decorated with complex 
engraved scenes and associated in modern scholarship with Late Bronze Age Aegean
iconography and funerary symbolism. It is most widely known through Arthur J. 
Evans’s 1925 study of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals 
from a rock-cut tomb near Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, which he interpreted as 
offering a "glimpse into the Minoan after-world" and to which he gave the 
Homeric name "Ring of Nestor".[9][39][41] A separate but related scholarly 
tradition identifies a gold signet ring from Kakovatos in Messenia, today in the
Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, as the "Ring of Nestor", dating it to the Late 
Minoan IB–II period (c. 1530–1400 BCE) and cataloguing it as one of the largest 
known Aegean signet rings.[1][2] The ring’s significance lies in its 
multi-register iconography, its contested authenticity and cultural attribution,
and its role as a comparative benchmark in the study of Aegean glyptic, funerary
beliefs, and the reception of Homeric figures in modern 
archaeology.[1][3][13][25]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', 
'20260628153402781836_4405eddf', '20260628154545738230_96f7b025', 
'20260628152124687708_7516438c', '20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', 
'20260628152124716216_d7950336', '20260628152251782676_93905c8c', 
'20260628152756345344_1e41be57'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Discovery 
and naming', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628153403719533_748a1f3a'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'Arthur J. Evans 
introduced the term "Ring of Nestor" in a monograph published in London in 1925,
titled *The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia*.[9][16][39] The work is structured in two main parts: an initial 
section describing a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals 
from a rock-cut Mycenaean tomb near Thisbê, Boeotia, and a second section in 
which Evans interprets the imagery on these rings as providing insight into a 
Minoan afterlife.[9][39][41]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', 
'20260628152407087140_b91ef2dd', '20260628153402781836_4405eddf', 
'20260628154545738230_96f7b025'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Evans 
describes the treasure as consisting of several gold signet rings and bead-seals
deposited in association with a rock-cut tomb near Thisbê, which he 
characterizes as Mycenaean.[9][41] Within this assemblage he singled out one 
elaborately engraved gold signet ring, noting its beaded hoop and complex 
scenes, and designated it the "Ring of Nestor"; he used this ring as the 
centerpiece for his discussion of what he called the "Minoan 
after-world".[9][41] The Heidelberg digital edition of his work associates 
keywords such as "Nestor", "Ring", "Minoan culture", "Thisvi", and "Kakóvatos", 
underscoring both the Boeotian findspot and the broader Minoan cultural frame in
which Evans placed the object.[9]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628154545738230_96f7b025'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Evans’s use of the Homeric name "Nestor" reflects his 
broader interest in connecting archaeological finds with figures and places 
known from Homeric epic, an approach visible in his later writings on the 
"Minoan world" and on Aegean prehistory.[12][24] Subsequent scholarship on 
Mycenaean Pylos and the so-called Palace of Nestor has maintained the Homeric 
association in site nomenclature, while treating Evans’s "Ring of Nestor" as a 
specific artefact whose name derives from his 1925 publication rather than from 
any ancient inscription.[21][37]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152251729228_5d864f18', '20260628152723952778_76dd4533', 
'20260628152407209397_09a29cba', '20260628152923068010_71ffad12'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Archaeological context and provenance', 
'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628153403719533_748a1f3a'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'In Evans’s 
reconstruction, the Ring of Nestor originally formed part of a sepulchral 
treasure recovered from a rock-cut chamber tomb near the modern village of 
Thísvi (ancient Thisbê) in Boeotia.[9][39][41] He presents the rings and 
bead-seals as grave goods belonging to a Mycenaean-period burial, emphasizing 
their association with a funerary context and using this setting as the 
framework for his discussion of the ring’s putative afterlife imagery.[9][41] 
Library catalogues and digital editions of the monograph consistently describe 
the treasure as coming from Thisbê, Boeotia, thereby anchoring the ring’s 
reported archaeological provenance in central Greece.[16][39]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628153402781836_4405eddf', 
'20260628154545738230_96f7b025', '20260628152407087140_b91ef2dd'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later Aegean and Pylos scholarship has continued to cite
Evans’s 1925 study as the standard reference for the Ring of Nestor and its 
Thisbê context, particularly when discussing the morphology of Aegean gold 
signet rings and their distribution.[40] In a study of the Griffin Warrior tomb 
at Pylos, Jack L. Davis notes that the bands of the Ring of Nestor and the 
so-called Ring of Minos are morphologically similar to those of the Griffin 
Warrior’s rings, and cites Evans’s fig. 42 as the canonical illustration of the 
Nestor ring.[40] This comparative use underscores the ring’s role as a reference
piece in discussions of Mycenaean elite burials and their adoption of 
Minoan-style signet rings.[17][40]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628153402847685_711d3e54', '20260628152407111081_271f8a03'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Popular and semi-scholarly accounts of the Griffin 
Warrior discoveries at Pylos sometimes refer to the Ring of Nestor as the only 
other major gold signet ring previously known from the Pylos area, noting that 
it had been purchased by Arthur Evans.[11][22][34] These narratives highlight 
regional connections between Pylos and other centres possessing elaborate signet
rings, but they do not alter the scholarly view that Evans’s Ring of Nestor was 
reported as coming from a Boeotian rock-cut tomb rather than from a Pylian 
excavation.[9][39][40]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152251706798_280e6a9f', 
'20260628152407230397_2a364cb6', '20260628152922947248_94dc4a70', 
'20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628153402781836_4405eddf', 
'20260628153402847685_711d3e54'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Later 
history and museum status', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids':
['20260628152126826160_4d600079', '20260628152845532620_9a5bb54a'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'A separate line of evidence derives from modern catalogues of Aegean 
glyptic, which identify a gold signet ring from Kakovatos in Messenia, described
as "Kakovatos (Messenia), gold signet ring (\'Ring of Nestor\'). L. 3.27 cm. LM 
IB–II (1530–1400 bc). Oxford, Ashmolean Museum 1938–1130", and treat this piece 
as the Ring of Nestor.[2] In this view, the ring now in the Ashmolean Museum 
under inventory number 1938.1130 is the object known in the literature as the 
Ring of Nestor, linking the name to a specific museum-held artefact from the 
western Peloponnese.[2]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152124687708_7516438c'], 
'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Vlachopoulos’s study of the ring reports that 
the Ashmolean gold signet, measuring approximately 3.29 × 2.18 cm, is the fifth 
largest Aegean signet ring known and that research has confirmed its 
authenticity.[1] He notes that the ring had been withdrawn from exhibition at 
certain points when doubts about its authenticity were prevalent, but that by 
1995 it was again placed on public display, indicating a renewed institutional 
confidence in the object.[1][28] A related article on the Ring of Nestor 
emphasizes that new technical observations support its authenticity and 
documents this return to exhibition.[28]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', '20260628152841748020_95f45291'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The inclusion of the Ring of Nestor in the *Corpus der 
minoischen und mykenischen Siegel* (CMS) as catalogue number 277 further anchors
it within the standard reference corpus of Minoan and Mycenaean seals and rings,
and an American Journal of Archaeology review of CMS volume VI discusses the 
ring in the context of authenticity debates.[3][4] The review notes that some 
scholars continue to harbour doubts about the authenticity of CMS no. 277 (the 
Ring of Nestor), even as others accept it, situating the ring among a small 
group of contentious pieces in the corpus.[4]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124716216_d7950336', '20260628152124746007_b5bc2226'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Scholarly discussions therefore differentiate between 
Evans’s Thisbê treasure as originally published and the Ashmolean ring from 
Kakovatos, while also acknowledging that the name "Ring of Nestor" has been 
applied to both the Boeotian-context ring described by Evans and the Kakovatos 
ring catalogued in CMS and in modern museum records.[1][2][9][39] This dual 
usage contributes to ongoing ambiguity in the literature regarding the ring’s 
precise archaeological provenance, even as the Ashmolean inventory number 
1938.1130 provides a concrete modern collection context.[1][2][28]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', '20260628152124687708_7516438c',
'20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628153402781836_4405eddf', 
'20260628152841748020_95f45291'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Physical 
description', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628152126753218_5108bfb5', '20260628154550905025_daabb80d'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is described in modern catalogues as a gold signet
ring with an oval bezel and a beaded hoop.[1][2][14] The beaded construction of 
the hoop has been noted as a distinctive feature, and a Cambridge epilogue on 
royal signet rings explicitly compares another ring’s "beaded gold hoop" and 
"three successive scenes" to those of the Ring of Nestor, indicating that its 
hoop form and multi-register layout have become a reference standard in the 
typology of Aegean beaded rings.[14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', '20260628152124687708_7516438c', 
'20260628152251811261_9fe900ea'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Vlachopoulos 
records that the ring measures approximately 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, placing it 
among the largest known Aegean signet rings, while a Cambridge glyptic chapter 
gives the length as 3.27 cm.[1][2] Both sources describe the ring as the fifth 
largest Aegean signet ring, and together they indicate that the oval bezel is 
about 2.18 cm in width, despite minor discrepancies in the reported 
length.[1][2]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', 
'20260628152124687708_7516438c'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The ring’s 
overall appearance is that of a massive gold hoop with a large, slightly convex 
bezel engraved with a complex scene that occupies multiple horizontal 
registers.[1][3][31] Scholars describe fourteen human figures distributed across
the composition, together with fantastic creatures, altars, architectural 
elements interpreted as sacred buildings, and various cult implements, creating 
a densely populated ritual landscape.[1][3][31] The combination of beaded hoop, 
large dimensions, and detailed multi-figure engraving contributes to its 
prominence within Aegean glyptic.[1][2][31]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', '20260628152124716216_d7950336', 
'20260628152841840115_dc815f56'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 
'Iconography', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628152758233283_d1321e9f', '20260628154551214211_e5aa16ec'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'Evans’s original discussion presents the Ring of Nestor’s engraving 
as a multi-register narrative scene combining processional and ritual 
elements.[9][41] He describes sequences of human figures moving towards or 
interacting with architectural structures, altars, and vegetation, and he 
emphasizes the presence of fantastic creatures, including a griffin, as markers 
of a sacral or otherworldly setting.[9][41] In his interpretation, the 
arrangement of figures and motifs suggests a journey or transition associated 
with the afterlife, which he links to what he terms the "Minoan 
after-world".[9][41]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', 
'20260628154545738230_96f7b025'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later 
iconographic analyses have sought to describe the scene in more neutral terms. A
study focusing on sacred sites in miniature on Minoan gold rings notes that in 
the Ring of Nestor a griffin appears seated on a throne or special seat, 
contributing to a composition that integrates sacred animals with cult 
architecture and human officiants.[31] The same study groups the ring with other
Minoan-style gold rings that depict complex sacred landscapes, interpreting them
as representations of ritual activities or epiphanic events rather than as 
literal depictions of an afterlife.[31]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152841840115_dc815f56'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'An article 
examining the Ring of Nestor’s iconography within the broader context of Aegean 
forgeries highlights the unusually dense and hybrid character of its motifs.[3] 
The author notes the juxtaposition of Minoan-style female figures, male figures 
in dynamic poses, and fantastic creatures with elements that resemble Egyptian 
iconographic conventions, such as certain headdresses and compositional 
devices.[3][13][25] This combination has contributed to debates over whether the
ring’s scene should be understood as a genuine Late Bronze Age Aegean 
composition, a modern pastiche drawing on Aegean and Egyptian models, or an 
intermediate case of "pseudo-Minoan" iconography.[13][25]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124716216_d7950336', '20260628152251782676_93905c8c', 
'20260628152756345344_1e41be57'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Dating and 
cultural attribution', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628154550986366_5384281c', '20260628154550905025_daabb80d', 
'20260628154551096189_a8a9cb09'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'Modern glyptic catalogues
date the Ring of Nestor to the Late Minoan IB–II period, corresponding 
approximately to 1530–1400 BCE, and associate it with a Kakovatos context in 
Messenia.[2] This dating places the ring within the broader horizon of Late 
Bronze Age Aegean elite artefacts, roughly contemporary with early Mycenaean 
palatial developments and with the period to which the Griffin Warrior tomb at 
Pylos is assigned.[2][17] Evans, writing earlier, had placed the Thisbê treasure
within a Late Minoan I framework on stylistic grounds, reflecting earlier 
chronological terminology.[41][45]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124687708_7516438c', '20260628152407111081_271f8a03', 
'20260628154545738230_96f7b025', '20260628154545848657_5bfdc764'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The ring’s cultural attribution has been debated. Evans 
considered the ring and related pieces as manifestations of a Minoan-influenced 
repertoire within a Mycenaean funerary context, emphasizing stylistic parallels 
with Cretan glyptic and seal imagery.[9][41] Subsequent scholarship has 
variously described the ring as Minoan, Mycenaean, or "Minoan-style", and the 
Cambridge chapter on Minoan glyptic lists it under gold signet rings 
demonstrating Minoan-style iconography in mainland contexts.[2][26]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628154545738230_96f7b025',
'20260628152124687708_7516438c', '20260628152756387361_b9faca71'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'One strand of scholarship characterizes the Ring of 
Nestor as "pseudo-Minoan" on the basis of its combination of Aegean and Egyptian
motifs and compositional traits.[13][25] In this perspective, the ring is seen 
as either a modern creation imitating Minoan glyptic using knowledge of Egyptian
art, or as an object whose precise chronological and cultural placement remains 
uncertain.[13][25] Other studies, including Vlachopoulos’s analysis and the 
Cambridge "Fakes and Dubitanda" chapter, argue that while doubts about the 
ring’s authenticity have a long history, recent technical and stylistic 
observations support its authenticity as a Bronze Age artefact, even if some 
scholars continue to regard it as a doubtful gem.[1][28][30]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152251782676_93905c8c', '20260628152756345344_1e41be57', 
'20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', '20260628152841748020_95f45291', 
'20260628152841807143_6101ab94'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 
'Interpretations and significance', 'section_type': 'section', 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628154551214211_e5aa16ec', 
'20260628152758233283_d1321e9f'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans framed the Ring of 
Nestor as providing a "glimpse into the Minoan after-world", interpreting its 
multi-figure scene as a representation of the journey of the soul and of 
funerary rites conducted in a Minoan-influenced ritual landscape.[9][41] He drew
comparisons between the ring’s motifs and those on seals from Crete, Mycenaean 
Greece, and Egypt, arguing that the ring offered visual evidence for beliefs 
about the afterlife in the Aegean Bronze Age and linking these to Homeric 
traditions about Nestor and his household.[9][12][41]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628152251729228_5d864f18', 
'20260628154545738230_96f7b025'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later 
iconographic studies have treated the ring as part of a wider corpus of 
Minoan-style gold rings depicting complex sacred landscapes, processions, and 
epiphanic scenes.[31] In this context, the Ring of Nestor is used to explore how
miniature images of shrines, thrones, sacred trees, and animals could encode 
information about ritual practice and the perception of sacred space.[31] These 
studies often focus on formal and spatial relationships within the composition, 
rather than on specific mythological narratives, and they highlight the ring’s 
value for understanding how ritual imagery circulated between Crete and the 
Mycenaean mainland.[31][26]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152841840115_dc815f56', 
'20260628152756387361_b9faca71'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Analyses of 
the ring’s iconography in relation to Aegean forgeries and Egyptian influence 
consider it a key case study for assessing the boundaries between authentic 
Bronze Age artefacts and later creations.[3][13][25] By comparing the Ring of 
Nestor’s motifs with Egyptian models and with securely provenanced Aegean rings,
these studies use it to test criteria for authenticity, such as engraving 
technique, wear patterns, and consistency with contemporary iconographic 
conventions.[3][25][30] The ring thus plays a role not only in discussions of 
Aegean religious imagery but also in methodological debates about how 
archaeologists and art historians evaluate contested objects.[3][5][30]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628152124716216_d7950336', '20260628152251782676_93905c8c',
'20260628152756345344_1e41be57', '20260628152124788632_ce48f530', 
'20260628152841807143_6101ab94'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In the context
of Mycenaean elite burials and the Griffin Warrior grave at Pylos, the Ring of 
Nestor has been cited as an earlier or parallel example of a large gold signet 
ring with complex ritual imagery, used as a benchmark for understanding the 
status and symbolism of the Griffin Warrior’s rings.[11][17][40] By serving as a
comparative point of reference for ring size, hoop construction, and 
iconographic themes, it contributes to broader interpretations of how Mycenaean 
elites adopted and adapted Minoan-style imagery to articulate authority, 
identity, and connections to the divine.[14][17][26]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152251706798_280e6a9f', '20260628152407111081_271f8a03', 
'20260628153402847685_711d3e54', '20260628152251811261_9fe900ea', 
'20260628152756387361_b9faca71'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Scholarly 
debates and controversies', 'section_type': 'section', 'section_type_display': 
None, 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628152126826160_4d600079', 
'20260628152758109782_1979f941', '20260628152845532620_9a5bb54a', 
'20260628154551096189_a8a9cb09', '20260628152758233283_d1321e9f'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor has long been the subject of debates over 
authenticity. Early scepticism led some scholars to classify it as a "gemma 
dubitanda"—a doubtful gem—reflecting uncertainties about whether it was 
genuinely of Bronze Age date or a later forgery.[5] Hudler’s discussion of 
"e-scaping arguments" uses the Ring of Nestor as a case in which doubts persist 
even when archaeological context and stylistic analyses are considered, 
illustrating how some objects remain contested despite extensive study.[5]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628152124788632_ce48f530'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 
'An article devoted to the Ring of Nestor’s iconography situates it within 
broader debates about Aegean forgeries and notes that its stylistic 
peculiarities have contributed to suspicions of modern manufacture.[3] The 
Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article "The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor 
Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography" characterizes the ring as "pseudo-Minoan" and
argues that its imagery draws heavily on Egyptian models, suggesting either a 
modern copyist or an object whose relationship to authentic Aegean glyptic is 
problematic.[13][25] At the same time, the Cambridge "Fakes and Dubitanda" 
chapter acknowledges that while the ring is treated there as an authentic 
object, doubts about it "have not ceased and probably never will", underscoring 
its liminal position between acceptance and scepticism.[30]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628152124716216_d7950336', '20260628152251782676_93905c8c', 
'20260628152756345344_1e41be57', '20260628152841807143_6101ab94'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In contrast, Vlachopoulos’s 2020 study presents new 
technical and stylistic observations that, in his view, support the authenticity
of the Ring of Nestor and argue against its classification as a forgery.[1] A 
separate article on the ring similarly reports that technical analyses support 
authenticity and notes that the ring was returned to public display in 1995, 
implying institutional acceptance of these conclusions.[28] Together, these 
works represent a strand of scholarship that, while acknowledging earlier 
doubts, regards the ring as a genuine Late Bronze Age artefact.[1][28]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628152124654716_343e1a7d', 
'20260628152841748020_95f45291'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'There is also 
debate over the ring’s cultural and stylistic affiliation. Evans interpreted the
scene as a window onto a Minoan afterlife with connections to Homeric 
narratives, whereas later studies such as "Traces of Places" emphasize its 
depiction of a ritual landscape without invoking specific Homeric 
stories.[9][31] Other scholars stress the strong Egyptian component in its 
iconography and describe it as "pseudo-Minoan", while still others treat it as 
part of a broader Minoan-style tradition adapted in Mycenaean 
contexts.[13][25][26][31] These divergent interpretations reflect different 
methodological priorities, from literary comparanda to formal iconographic 
analysis and cross-cultural stylistic comparison.[9][13][25][31]', 'source_ids':
['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', '20260628152841840115_dc815f56', 
'20260628152251782676_93905c8c', '20260628152756345344_1e41be57', 
'20260628152756387361_b9faca71'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Finally, the 
ring’s provenance and naming have generated some confusion. While Evans 
consistently describes his Ring of Nestor as part of a treasure from a rock-cut 
tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, modern glyptic catalogues and the Ashmolean 
inventory associate a "Ring of Nestor" with a gold signet ring from Kakovatos in
Messenia.[2][9][16][39] Popular treatments of the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos 
sometimes present the Ring of Nestor as an earlier major ring from the Pylos 
area acquired by Evans, contributing to perceptions that the ring is linked to 
Pylos.[11][18][19] Scholarly treatments, however, generally distinguish between 
Evans’s Boeotian-context ring and the Ashmolean ring from Kakovatos, while 
acknowledging that the shared name "Ring of Nestor" has led to ambiguity in the 
literature.[1][2][40]', 'source_ids': ['20260628152251657830_e85ded99', 
'20260628152407087140_b91ef2dd', '20260628153402781836_4405eddf', 
'20260628152124687708_7516438c', '20260628152251706798_280e6a9f', 
