   signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia" (pp. 1–42) and Part II "A  
   glimpse into the Minoan after-world" (pp. 43–75). [1]                        
 • The Heidelberg metadata lists keywords including "Nestor; Ring; Minoische    
   Kultur; Thísvi; Europe; Greece; Kakóvatos," indicating thematic focus on a   
   ring associated with Nestor, Minoan culture, and the Boeotian site of        
   Thísvi/Thisbê. [1]                                                           
 • Evans describes the treasure as consisting of multiple gold signet rings and 
   bead-seals found in connection with a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near Thisbê in 
   Boeotia. [1]                                                                 
 • Evans singles out one particular gold signet ring from the treasure, assigns 
   it the name "the ring of Nestor," and discusses its beaded hoop and elaborate
   engraved scenes as central to his interpretation of the Minoan after-world.  
   [1]                                                                          
 • In Part II, Evans uses motifs and compositions on the Nestor ring and related
   rings to propose representations of an afterworld or funerary journey,       
   comparing them with seal imagery from Crete, Mycenaean Greece, and Egyptian  
   motifs. [1]                                                                  
 • The Heidelberg scroll-view lists the contents of Evans’s work as             
   "Titelblatt"; "1–42 I. Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and          
   bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia"; "43–75 II. A glimpse into the Minoan       
   after-world"; and "Tafel I – V" (plates). [2]                                
 • The scroll-view interface identifies the work as digitized historic          
   literature within Heidelberg University Library’s digital collections and    
