   bead-seals, including the ring designated by Evans as the "ring of Nestor,"  
   are available. [2]                                                           
 • The Griffin Warrior article states that a particular gold signet ring from   
   Tholos IV is one of the notable Mycenaean gold rings and that "the only other
   from this area was the famous Ring of Nestor – purchased by Arthur Evans,    
   famous for his work at Knossos." [3]                                         
 • Evans’s 1936 lecture notes a lion-spearing scene in "the later group at      
   Thisbê" and compares the spear-shaft with signet rings from Mycenae and other
   Boeotian gold objects. [4]                                                   
 • The lecture mentions a gold bead obtained in 1910 from a Boeotian peasant and
