 • Evans describes a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia     
   where a peasant from the nearby village of Dombrena discovered in 1915 a     
   hoard comprising multiple gold signet-rings, gold bead-seals, bronze         
   spear-heads, and a short sword of very late Mycenaean type. [21]             
 • In presenting the hoard from the Thisbê tomb, Evans singles out a large gold 
   signet ring with a beaded hoop and a bezel bearing three successive miniature
   relief scenes, which he labels "the Ring of Nestor" and treats as the        
   centerpiece of the treasure. [21]                                            
 • Evans uses the combination of gold rings, bead-seals, bronze spear-heads, and
   a short sword of very late Mycenaean type from the Thisbê tomb to argue that 
   the burial belongs to a late phase of the Mycenaean period. [21]             
 • Evans interprets the three-panel imagery on the Ring of Nestor’s bezel as    
   depicting stages of a passage to the afterworld, drawing parallels with      
   Minoan religious motifs and arguing that the ring reflects Minoan beliefs    
   adapted in a Mycenaean funerary context. [21]                                
 • The Heidelberg digital monograph outlines sections titled "Sepulchral        
   treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia" and "A    
   glimpse into the Minoan after-world" and includes plates I–V illustrating the
   Ring of Nestor and related objects. [2]                                      
 • In the monograph, Evans compares the Ring of Nestor’s beaded band and scene  
   layout to other notable gold rings, including the so-called Ring of Minos,   
   and suggests that similarities in band form and engraving technique indicate 
   shared workshops or stylistic traditions. [2]                                
 • The HathiTrust catalog entry for "The ring of Nestor" records the title as   
   including the subtitle "a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a         
