 • The discovery in 2015 of the Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace of Nestor 
   at Pylos revealed a Late Helladic IIA (approximately 1450–1400 BCE)          
   stone‑built tomb containing hundreds of grave goods, including four gold     
   signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes, which have been interpreted
   as evidence of close connections between the Mycenaean elite at Pylos and    
   Minoan culture. [3][22][27]                                                  
 • Evans’s study of the Ring of Nestor and associated material is structured in 
   two main parts: a descriptive account of the sepulchral treasure of gold     
   signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê and an interpretive section in which 
   he reads the imagery, including that on the Ring of Nestor, as providing     
   insight into a putative Minoan afterlife. [1]                                
