   that likely also applies to the Ring of Nestor. [30]                         
 • Archaeologists Jack L. Davis and Sharon R. Stocker present the Griffin       
   Warrior grave and related Mycenaean tholos tombs at Pylos as discoveries that
   significantly illuminate the emergence of Mycenaean civilization and the     
   development of early complex or state‑level societies in Europe. [31]        
 • Popular and blog‑level discussions of the Griffin Warrior grave and its      
   "Pylos Combat Agate" emphasize the extraordinary fineness and anatomical     
   sophistication of the miniature combat scene’s engraving, sometimes claiming 
   that it rivals Classical Greek art produced roughly a millennium later and   
   suggesting that it could reshape narratives of the development of Greek art, 
   although such claims are hyperbolic and not presented as a scholarly         
