 • The catalogue entry identifies the Ring of Nestor as a gold signet ring from 
   Kakovatos in Messenia and records its length as L. 3.27 cm, assigning it to  
   Late Minoan IB–II (c. 1530–1400 BC) and listing its museum inventory as      
   Oxford, Ashmolean Museum 1938.1130, with a reference to I. Pini, OJA 17      
   (1998), 3. [9]                                                               
 • Vlachopoulos describes the Ring of Nestor as a Late Bronze Age gold signet   
   ring in the Ashmolean Museum, giving its dimensions as 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and
   noting that it is the fifth largest Aegean signet ring. [8]                  
 • Vlachopoulos notes a 'dark film' on the surface of the Ring of Nestor’s gold 
   and discusses this in connection with earlier descriptions that treated the  
   film as decoration. [8]                                                      
 • In his analysis of the bezel scene, Vlachopoulos describes a central female  
   figure, a griffin, architectural elements, altars, and trees composing a     
   complex ritual or epiphany scene on the Ring of Nestor. [8]                  
 • Vlachopoulos reviews previous scholarly claims that the Ring of Nestor might 
   be a forgery due to stylistic anomalies and questionable provenance but      
   concludes, after comparing the ring to securely provenanced examples, that   
   the ring is authentic and dates to Late Minoan I–II (16th–15th c. BC). [8]   
 • Evans’s 1925 study describes the Ring of Nestor as a large gold signet ring  
   with an engraved scene including human figures, a griffin, altars, trees, and
   architectural forms, and notes a dark film over parts of its gold surface    
   that earlier observers took as decoration. [41]                              
 • Evans reports that the Ring of Nestor was said to have been found near       
   Kakovatos, close to Pylos in Messenia, and that he acquired it through an    
   antiquities dealer, using this provenance to associate the ring with Nestor’s
   Pylos. [41]                                                                  
 • Evans dates the Ring of Nestor to the later phase of Minoan art,             
   approximately Late Minoan I, and argues that its style and iconography are   
   characteristically Minoan even though it was reportedly found on the         
   mainland. [41]                                                               
 • Evans interprets the Ring of Nestor’s scene as a glimpse into the Minoan     
   afterworld and an epiphany of a deity, drawing parallels with Homeric        
   descriptions of Nestor’s wealth and religious observances. [41]              
 • The Journal of Hellenic Studies article by Evans reiterates the ring’s       
   characterization as a very large gold signet ring of Late Minoan date from   
   the area of Pylos, with a complex religious scene engraved on its bezel. [21]
 • The iconography paper summarizes prior claims that the Ring of Nestor could  
   be a forgery, discusses stylistic and iconographic grounds for these claims, 
   and then compares the ring with securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean    
   rings to evaluate these arguments. [5]                                       
 • In examining the Ring of Nestor’s scene, the iconography paper describes     
   figures, animals, structures, altars, and trees and places these elements    
   within the broader repertoire of Aegean glyptic imagery. [5]                 
 • The iconography paper notes that debates about the Ring of Nestor’s          
   authenticity influence how scholars interpret its iconography and its        
   significance within the Minoan and Mycenaean glyptic corpus. [5]             
 • The Griffin Warrior feature states that among the few previously known gold  
   signet rings linked to Pylos was the famous Ring of Nestor, purchased by     
   Arthur Evans, and that this ring has long been emblematic of elite Late      
   Bronze Age culture at Pylos. [3]                                             
 • The UCLA event listing caption identifies the 'Ring of Nestor', c. 1500 BC,  
   Oxford, Ashmolean Museum, as a key image illustrating connections between    
   Pylos and Minoan Crete. [42]                                                 
 • The 'Lord of the Gold Rings' paper cites Evans’s remark that the Ring of     
   Nestor has a dark film over the gold surface that was regarded as decoration,
   and the author compares this observation with the surfaces of other gold     
   rings. [22]                                                                  
 • The 'Lord of the Gold Rings' paper states that the bands of the Ring of      
   Nestor and the Ring of Minos are strikingly similar to the bands of some     
   newly discovered gold rings, suggesting consistent manufacturing practices.  
   [22]                                                                         
 • Hudler uses the example of Evans’s 'Ring of Nestor' publication to show how  
   digital access to classic works enables renewed debates on the authenticity  
   of artifacts like the Ring of Nestor and shapes the way arguments are        
   constructed online. [11]                                                     
 • The 'Traces of Places' article cites the Ring of Nestor as an example where a
   sacred seat or throne on the ring’s bezel serves as a seat for a griffin,    
   illustrating how such elements represent miniature sacred sites on Minoan    
   gold rings. [29]                                                             
 • In its broader argument, the 'Traces of Places' article interprets the Ring  
   of Nestor’s scene as a ritual landscape composed of seats, animals, altars,  
   trees, and architectural elements that encode sacred places in miniature on  
   the ring’s surface. [29]                                                     
 • The Ring of Nestor is a large Late Bronze Age gold signet ring, now in the   
   Ashmolean Museum in Oxford (inv. 1938.1130), reportedly from Kakovatos near  
   Pylos in Messenia. [8][9][3][21][41][42]                                     
 • The Ring of Nestor measures roughly 3.27–3.29 cm in length and about 2.18 cm 
   in the other axis, making it one of the largest known Aegean gold signet     
   rings (described as the fifth largest in the corpus). [8][9][41]             
 • The bezel of the Ring of Nestor carries a complex engraved scene including   
   multiple human figures (with a central female figure), a griffin, altars,    
   trees, architectural elements, and a sacred seat or throne that serves as a  
   perch for the griffin, arranged as a miniature ritual landscape or sacred    
   site. [8][5][29][41]                                                         
 • The surface of the Ring of Nestor’s gold is partly covered by a dark film or 
   patina, which early observers, including Evans, described as decorative, and 
   which later scholars discuss as a surface condition when comparing it to     
   other gold rings. [8][22][41]                                                
 • Stylistic and iconographic analysis places the Ring of Nestor within the     
   Minoan glyptic tradition, even though it was reportedly found on the Greek   
   mainland near Pylos. [8][9][21][41][42]                                      
 • Modern scholarship generally dates the Ring of Nestor to the Late Minoan I–II
   (or LM IB–II) phase of the Late Bronze Age, approximately the 16th–15th      
   centuries BC (c. 1530–1400 BC), consistent with Evans’s earlier assignment to
   Late Minoan I. [8][9][5][21][41][42]                                         
 • Scholars commonly interpret the Ring of Nestor’s bezel scene as a Minoan     
   ritual or epiphany scene, sometimes linked to the afterlife, comprising a    
   ritual landscape of seats, altars, trees, architectural elements, and a      
   griffin around a central female figure. [8][21][29][41]                      
 • The Ring of Nestor’s manufacture appears consistent with Aegean gold ring    
   production, with its band and bezel morphology closely resembling those of   
   other securely provenanced rings such as the so‑called Ring of Minos and some
   more recently discovered examples. [5][22]                                   
 • Despite long‑standing doubts that the Ring of Nestor might be a modern       
   forgery—based on stylistic anomalies and uncertain provenance—the current    
   scholarly consensus, following detailed stylistic comparison with securely   
   provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean rings, regards the ring as authentic.       
   [8][5][11]                                                                   
 • The Ring of Nestor has played a prominent role in discussions of Late Bronze 
   Age elite culture at Pylos and in linking Pylian archaeology with Minoan art,
   serving as a key example of a prestigious gold signet ring associated (by    
   report and later scholarship) with Nestor’s Pylos. [8][3][21][41][42]        

